Task:
Figure out whether a set of squares can be packed into any rectangular region.
The side lengths of the squares are powers of 2.


Complexity and Limits:

The sum of the areas of the squares is a.
Runtime: O(sqrt(a) log a)
Limits: 1 <= a <= 10^{15}
        (This allows a largest square of size 2^24)


Solution:
Calculate the sum of the areas of all squares.
Try all possible rectangles of the appropriate size. There are less than sqrt(a) options.
Now iterate over the squares sizes, starting with the largest.
All squares of size 2^i and larger fit exactly if the rectangle with its side lengths rounded down to multiples of 2^i has a large enough area to fit the squares of this size and all larger squares.


Difficulty:
60%
